# Normalize to LF in the repository regardless of the committer's platform:
# these are source files, and CRLF churn would make every diff noisy.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ...except Windows batch files, which are stored as CRLF IN THE REPOSITORY.
#
# `-text` (not `text eol=crlf`) is deliberate. The latter normalizes the blob to
# LF and only converts on checkout — but GitHub serves raw.githubusercontent.com
# straight from the blob, so the "download install.cmd" link in the README would
# hand every user an LF file. cmd.exe misparses an LF-only batch file: it runs
# past `exit /b` and starts executing the text below it as commands. Verified by
# running an LF copy of this exact script and watching it fail with
# "'-------------------------------' is not recognized" while the CRLF copy
# installed cleanly. `-text` stores the bytes as they are, so both the clone and
# the raw download get a working file.
#
# Cost: git treats it as binary, so `git diff` shows "Binary files differ"
# rather than a line diff. For one rarely-edited installer that is the right
# trade — a broken download is much worse than a coarse diff.
*.cmd -text

# Binary-ish / generated artifacts that must never be line-ending converted.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.mp4 binary
*.7z binary
